1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,599
The baggage-tracking system at Gate West
started as one WAR. Tag scanners, carousel
boards, lost-bag claims, overnight
reconciliation — all in one deployable.

2
00:00:11,719 --> 00:00:25,039
Three years later, a carousel outage still
forced a full redeploy of claims and
reconciliation. The monolith was not just
"big." It was one classpath, one restart
boundary, and one blame surface.

3
00:00:25,159 --> 00:00:29,999
When the team finally split modules,
Spring became the awkward part of the
conversation.

4
00:00:30,119 --> 00:00:42,319
Someone had pulled a vague "spring"
dependency into the reconciliation JAR and
dragged servlet APIs, MVC, and messaging
into a nightly batch that never served
HTTP. Build times grew.

5
00:00:42,439 --> 00:00:53,599
On-call could not tell which Spring pieces
were load-bearing. The failure was modular
blindness, not a missing annotation.
Spring Framework is layered so you can
compose different shapes for different
jobs.

6
00:00:53,719 --> 00:01:02,200
At the center sit spring-core, spring-
beans, and spring-context — the container
that understands bean definitions,
factories, and the application context.

7
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:14,879
Everything else is optional: you add a
ring only when that deployable needs it.
Watch how that maps onto Gate West after
the split. Scanner ingest is a headless
process that receives bag-tag events.

8
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:28,280
Its classpath needs the container plus
messaging — not MVC. The carousel board is
an HTTP service; it adds spring-webmvc
(and whatever view or JSON stack you
choose) on top of the same container core.

9
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:41,480
Overnight reconciliation is a batch job
with JDBC access to the bags database:
container plus spring-jdbc / transaction
support — and deliberately nothing from
the web stack. Same Spring family.

10
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:52,480
Three different module graphs. Three
restart boundaries. Look at this java for
a moment. At runtime that job never opens
port 8080, and the container does not
care.

11
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:58,480
It reads bean-definition metadata,
registers types in a BeanFactory, and
materializes the graph.

12
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:08,920
ApplicationContext sits on top of that
factory with events, messages, and
resource loading — which the carousel UI
will lean on later, and which the batch
job may barely touch.

13
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:18,120
Higher-level Spring modules may depend on
the container; your baggage domain should
not sprawl into every Spring package just
because a transitive dependency made it
available.

14
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:27,760
The architecture misconception that bit
Gate West is simple: "If the app uses
Spring, it uses Spring Web." False. Spring
is a modular toolbox.

15
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:34,759
A classpath that includes MVC in a
headless reconciler is an architecture
smell, not proof you needed a controller.

16
00:02:34,879 --> 00:02:46,960
Another smell: one shared "utils" module
that imports every Spring starter so every
deployable inherits everyone else's stack.
After the split, Gate West could restart
carousel boards without bouncing claims.

17
00:02:47,079 --> 00:02:58,599
But a quieter problem remained inside each
module: services still constructed
collaborators with new, and tests still
fought construction order. Who actually
owns object creation once the module
boundaries are drawn?
